   232	  `reverse`, `min`, `max`, `sum`, `first` or `last`. The refusal for any of them
   233	  is `src/interpreter.rs:2411`, which formats `Unknown function '{}'`.
   234	- **What is missing**: `sort`, `sort_by`, `reverse`, `min`, `max`, `sum`,
   235	  `mean`, `median`, `any`, `all`.
   236	- **Why**: every report ends in an ordering. "Top five", "p90", "most common
   237	  word" and "sorted by name then by id" are the last line of the program, and the
   238	  language cannot write that line.
   239	
   240	```redblue
   241	say sort([3, 1, 2])
   242	```
   243	
   244	Refused as `RuntimeError: Unknown function 'sort'`.
   245	
   246	## 5. No slicing or searching of text
   247	
   248	- **Blocked programs**: 4 — 21, 23, 24, 25 (at survey time; 23 since phase-101)
   249	- **Implementable**: implementable-in-Redblue
   250	- **Evidence**: `src/stdlib.rs:165` — the `text` row now carries the ten original
   251	  names plus `wrap`, `diff`, `dedent`, `template`, `pad` and `align`, none of
   252	  which takes an index or produces a substring. `split` is documented at
   253	  `src/stdlib.rs:2100` as taking a literal separator, and `src/stdlib.rs:817` is
   254	  the comment explaining why there is no pattern to give it. `length` counts
   255	  **characters**, not bytes (`src/runtime.rs:1522`), so a slicing design has a
   256	  defined unit to slice by; the gap is that no slicing or searching call exists.
   257	- **What phase-101 changed**: `text.wrap` wraps to a width, `text.dedent` removes
   258	  common indentation, `text.template` substitutes `<<name>>` placeholders,
   259	  `text.pad` and `text.align` produce fixed widths, and `text.diff` produces a
   260	  standard unified diff. Program 23 is therefore no longer blocked by padding.
   261	- **What is still missing**: `slice`, `substring`, `index_of`, `last_index_of`,
   262	  `count`, `repeat`, `chars`, `bytes`, `title`, `capitalize`.
   263	- **Why**: "everything after the last separator" and "the text between two
   264	  delimiters" are not exotic. They are the extraction half of programs 21, 24
   265	  and 25, and each still needs a `split`-and-rejoin loop that throws away the
   266	  information it needs.
   267	
   268	```redblue
   269	say text.slice("a1b22c", 1, 2)
   270	```
   271	
   272	Refused as `RuntimeError: Module 'text' has no function 'slice'`.
   273	
   274	## 6. No date or time arithmetic, and one parseable date format
   275	
   276	- **Blocked programs**: 4 — 2, 9, 10, 11 (at survey time; 0 since phase-097)
   277	- **Implementable**: binding-required
   278	- **Rust crate**: `chrono`
   279	- **Evidence**: `src/stdlib.rs:193` — the `time` row carries the date and
   280	duration surface added by phase-096 (`date`, `datetime`, `duration`, and the six
   281	`add_*` movers), plus `time.parse` added by phase-097. `src/runtime.rs:720`
   282	parses the ISO-8601 forms, including an offset and a fraction, into the date
   283	value; `src/runtime.rs:1708` formats a date or a numeric timestamp. `time.unix`
   284	now reads the same parser and also accepts a date value, so a birthdate read
   285	from text can enter the arithmetic. `time.from_unix` remains the numeric inverse.
   286	- **What phase-097 changed**: text parsing and caller-chosen formatting are now
   287	one date model. `time.parse` accepts a date-only or date-and-time ISO-8601 text,
   288	optionally with `.fraction`, `Z` or `±HH:MM`; a zone-free text is read as UTC.
   289	Malformed text is a catchable `Runtime` error naming the byte position.
   290	- **Note**: `chrono` is already a dependency (`Cargo.toml:32`) and already used
   291	  at `src/runtime.rs:720`. The gap was the surface, not the crate.
   292	- **Why**: age in years, "3 days ago", a month-end report and a file renamed by
   293	  its date all need arithmetic. A language that can read the clock but cannot move
   294	  it is a language that can log and not schedule.
   295	
   296	```redblue
   297	set born to time.parse("1990-03-04T00:00:00Z")
   298	say time.add_days(born, 365)
   299	```
   300	
   301	## 7. No directories, no globbing, no stat
   302	
   303	- **Blocked programs**: 4 — 2, 6, 12, 13
   304	- **Implementable**: binding-required
   305	- **Rust crate**: `walkdir`, `glob`
   306	- **Evidence**: `src/stdlib.rs:113` — the `files` row is eight names, every one of
   307	  them taking a whole path as text: `read`, `write`, `append`, `exists`, `lines`,
   308	  `delete`, `copy`, `rename`. There is no `list_dir`, no `mkdir`, no `glob`, no
   309	  `size`, no `modified`. Nothing in the language can ask what is in a directory:
   310	  `src/stdlib.rs:113` is the whole of the module.
   311	- **Why**: four programs in the sample start with "go and look at the files".
   312	  A script that cannot enumerate a directory cannot process a repository, a
   313	  downloads folder, or a log directory — which is most of what scripts are for.
   314	  `network` has no timeout control either (`src/stdlib.rs:144`), so program 29 is
   315	  blocked here as well.
   316	
   317	```redblue
   318	say files.glob("corpus/*.rb")
   319	```
   320	
   321	Refused as `RuntimeError: Module 'files' has no function 'glob'`.
   322	
   323	## 8. No encodings and no digests
   324	
   325	- **Blocked programs**: 3 — 20, 21, 30
   326	- **Implementable**: binding-required
   327	- **Rust crate**: `base64`, `hex`, `percent-encoding`, `sha2`
   328	- **Evidence**: `src/stdlib.rs:81` — the `base64`, `hex`, `hash` and `uuid`
   329	  rows now bind the `base64`, `hex`, `sha2` and `uuid` crates. The dependency
   330	  block in `Cargo.toml` holds all four.
   331	- **Why**: base64 auth headers, percent-encoded URLs and content hashes are
   332	  interoperability, not convenience. They are also the parts of "read this API"
   333	  that cannot be worked around by any amount of loop.
   334	- **Note**: phase-104 closed the encoding, digest and UUID parts of this rank.
   335	  `percent-encoding` remains an open binding entry, so the rank is not entirely
   336	  closed.
   337	
   338	```redblue
   339	say base64.decode("aGk=")
   340	```
   341	
   342	Answers `[104, 105]` since phase-104.
   343	
   344	## 9. No number formatting or number parsing
   345	
   346	- **Blocked programs**: 2 — 10, 22
   347	- **Implementable**: implementable-in-Redblue
   348	- **Evidence**: `src/stdlib.rs:499` and `src/stdlib.rs:501` — `to_text` and
   349	  `to_number` are the only two conversions between a number and anything else,
   350	  and neither takes a second argument. There is no precision, no base, no
   351	  thousands separator and no padding. The nearest thing to formatting is
   352	  `src/stdlib.rs:2255`, `to_text(any) -> text`, which is the default rendering.
   353	- **Why**: "print $1,234.50" is the last line of a money program, and
   354	  "parse the integer after the marker" is a step of program 10. Both need the
   355	  same thing — a format the caller states.
   356	
   357	```redblue
   358	say to_fixed(1234.5, 2)
   359	```
   360	
   361	Refused as `RuntimeError: Unknown function 'to_fixed'`.
   362	
   363	## 10. No writer for structured formats
   364	
   365	- **Blocked programs**: 2 — 5, 27
   366	- **Implementable**: implementable-in-Redblue
   367	- **Evidence**: `src/stdlib.rs:111` — the `csv` row is one name, `parse`.
   368	  `src/stdlib.rs:118` — `json` has both directions, and
   369	  `src/stdlib.rs:2379` gives `json.stringify`'s whole shape —
   370	  `json_stringify(any) -> text` — so it takes no indent argument, so the output is one line regardless.
   371	- **Why**: reading data is solved and writing it back is not. A program that
   372	  normalises, filters or summarises a CSV has nowhere to put its result. The
   373	  asymmetry is worse than an absence, because the parse half is documented in
   374	  `SPEC.md` and the write half reads as though it exists.
   375	
   376	```redblue
   377	say csv.stringify([["a", 1], ["b", 2]])
   378	```
   379	
   380	Refused as `RuntimeError: Module 'csv' has no function 'stringify'`.
   381	
   382	## Not surveyed
   383	
   384	The gaps below the line, with the same one-line evidence, so a reader who thinks
   385	something is missing can check rather than ask. They are not ranked: each blocks
   386	one program or fewer in the sample, and the tie-break rule puts the ranked
   387	entries first.
   388	
   389	| Gap | Evidence | Blocks |
   390	|---|---|---|
   391	| No `sys` surface beyond `argv` and CLI flags: no environment variables, exit code, cwd, platform | `src/stdlib.rs:163` | 31, 32 |
   392	| No set algebra: `unique`, `flatten`, `reverse`, intersection | `src/stdlib.rs:137` | 3 |
   393	| No statistics or integer maths: no `min`/`max`, `sum`, `mean`, `gcd`, `log2`, inverse trig | `src/stdlib.rs:139` | 8 |
   394	| No token generation beyond `uuid.generate`; `random_choice`/`random_shuffle` exist but no token-shaped call | `src/stdlib.rs:654` | 28 |
   395	| No concurrency, no timeout or retry control on `network.get` | `src/stdlib.rs:144` | 29 |
   396	| No `inspect`-style pretty printer; no assertion library beyond `expect`/`assert` | `src/stdlib.rs:626` | — |
   397	| No TOML/YAML/XML reader or writer | `src/stdlib.rs:19` | — |
   398	
   399	Four limits of this survey, stated so they are not mistaken for more:
   400	
   401	- **The sample is 32 mundane programs, not the world's software.** A gap that
   402	  blocks nothing here can still be the only thing missing from someone's project.
   403	  The ranking is a tie-break, not a proof of importance.
   404	- **Blocked counts overlap.** A program can be listed under several gaps, so the
   405	  counts do not sum to 32 and are not meant to.
   406	- **Nothing here is about the language surface.** Closures, `for each`, records,
   407	  objects and `try` are all present and are not assessed. `modules/*.rb` can
   408	  already add pure-language functions, which is why so many ranks are labelled
   409	  `implementable-in-Redblue`.
   410	- **This survey changed no line under `src/` when phase-090 wrote it.** Where a
   411	  row here could be fixed by one file in `modules/`, that remains a
   412	  recommendation. Phase-101 has since implemented the text operations noted
   413	  under rank 5; the other entries are unchanged.
